Rem ==================================================================
Rem builtinsurfacetest.shoddy — every builtin the runtime dispatches,
Rem accounted for against the seeded dictionary
Rem
Rem Assert-driven, in the cuttletest / reckonertest / seedtest pattern:
Rem it prints what it is doing and stops the run at the first assertion
Rem that fails, ending with ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED when it does not.
Rem
Rem WHAT THIS IS FOR. The reckoner's dictionary and the runtime's
Rem builtins are two different surfaces, and nothing until now compared
Rem them. A builtin is reachable from a prompt only if the core or some
Rem seed registered a word for it, and 148 builtins against a 565-word
Rem dictionary is not a comparison anybody makes by eye. So it is made
Rem here, once, and it partitions the whole surface three ways:
Rem
Rem   CLAIMED  — the core or a seed already registers this name.
Rem   EXCLUDED — deliberately absent, and the rule that excludes it is
Rem              named beside it below.
Rem   FREE     — neither, so this is what a builtin seed would be for.
Rem
Rem THE PARTITION IS THE TEST. Every name is in exactly one of the
Rem three, the three add up to the whole surface, and both of the
Rem interesting halves are asserted rather than printed: an EXCLUDED
Rem name must NOT be in the dictionary (a rule that says "never seed
Rem this" is worth nothing unless something checks), and the FREE set
Rem must be exactly what is written down. Steal a builtin name in some
Rem future seed and this file fails and names it.
Rem
Rem WHY IT IS WORTH HAVING BEFORE THE SEED IT IS FOR. Writing a seed
Rem over three dozen of the commonest names in the language — LEN, VAL,
Rem NTH, SORT, STR — is exactly the work where a silent collision is
Rem easy and expensive: a word registered over one another seed already
Rem owns simply replaces it, and the seed whose word vanished is the one
Rem that breaks, somewhere else, later. Settling the free list first
Rem makes that seed mechanical to write.
Rem
Rem THE DICTIONARY IT COMPARES AGAINST is halifax's, because halifax is
Rem the mill that folds in every seed there is, so its dictionary is the
Rem largest one the tree can build. The single exception is seedisam,
Rem which no mill can fold without a table handle to close over — and
Rem which registers no name that is also a builtin, so it changes
Rem nothing here.
Rem
Rem GROUND TRUTH, AND WHERE IT CAME FROM. Builtins() below is the
Rem dispatch in src/Shoddy.Runtime/Engine.cs, which is the only place
Rem the list exists. It is copied rather than derived because Shoddy
Rem cannot read its own runtime's source; the cost is that a builtin
Rem added there and not here is invisible, and the answer to that is
Rem that adding a builtin is a runtime change and this file is part of
Rem what a runtime change touches.
Rem
Rem Run: bin\mill run tst\builtinsurfacetest.shoddy   (from the repo root)
Rem ==================================================================

Include "../machines/reckoner.shoddy"
Include "../machines/seq.shoddy"
Include "../machines/str.shoddy"
Include "../mills/halifax/halifax-core.shoddy"

Rem ---- the runtime's whole dispatch, in one place ----------------------

Rem Split across several Defs only because one list literal of 148
Rem strings is a wall nobody can read; the grouping is alphabetical
Rem convenience and carries no meaning.
Def Builtins() As List Of String
    Concat(Concat(BuiltinsA(), BuiltinsB()), Concat(BuiltinsC(), BuiltinsD()))

Def BuiltinsA() As List Of String
    { "&", "*", "+", "-", "/", "<", "<=", "<>", "=", ">", ">=",
      "ABS", "ACOS", "AND", "APPENDFILE", "ARGS", "ASC", "ASIN", "ASSERT",
      "ATN", "ATN2", "BCLOSE", "BETAI", "BOPEN", "BPOS", "BSIZE", "CALL",
      "CEIL", "CHR", "CLOCK", "CONCAT", "COS", "DELETEFILE", "DEPTH", "DIM",
      "DROP", "DUP", "EACH", "ERF", "ERROR", "EXP" }

Def BuiltinsB() As List Of String
    { "FALSE", "FILEEXISTS", "FILTER", "FIRST", "FIX", "FLOOR", "FOLD",
      "GAMMAP", "GETBOOL", "GETNUM", "GETSTR", "IFTE", "INKEY", "INPUT",
      "INPUTLINE", "INSTR", "ISEMPTY", "ISNUMERIC", "LEFT", "LEN", "LENGTH",
      "LOG", "LOG10", "LOWER", "MAP", "MAX", "MID", "MIN", "MOD", "NEGATE",
      "NIP", "NOT", "NOTEOFF", "NOTEON", "NTH", "OR", "OVER", "PI" }

Def BuiltinsC() As List Of String
    { "PREPEND", "PRINT", "PUTBOOL", "PUTNUM", "PUTSTR", "RANGE", "READFILE",
      "REST", "REVERSE", "RIGHT", "RND", "ROT", "ROUND",
      "SCRIBBLERBLIT", "SCRIBBLERCLOSE", "SCRIBBLERFILL", "SCRIBBLERGETPIXEL",
      "SCRIBBLERHEIGHT", "SCRIBBLEROPEN", "SCRIBBLERPIXEL", "SCRIBBLERPLACE",
      "SCRIBBLERPOLL", "SCRIBBLERSAVE", "SCRIBBLERSETINTERVAL", "SCRIBBLERTEXT",
      "SCRIBBLERTITLE", "SCRIBBLERWAIT", "SCRIBBLERWIDTH",
      "SEED", "SEEK", "SETNTH", "SGN", "SIN", "SLEEP", "SORT" }

Def BuiltinsD() As List Of String
    { "SOUND", "SOUNDGAIN", "SOUNDQUEUE", "SOUNDSTOP", "SOUNDWAVE", "SQR",
      "STR", "SWAP", "TAN", "TANH", "TCPACCEPT", "TCPCLOSE", "TCPCONNECT",
      "TCPEOF", "TCPLISTEN", "TCPPEER", "TCPPOLL", "TCPRECV", "TCPSECURE",
      "TCPSEND", "TICKS", "TIMES", "TOARRAY", "TOLIST", "TRUE", "TRYREADFILE",
      "TRYWRITEFILE", "TUCK", "UPPER", "VAL", "VALOR", "WRAP", "WRITEFILE", "^" }

Rem ---- what is deliberately absent, and the rule that decides --------

Rem A rule and the names it covers. The rule travels WITH the names
Rem rather than in a comment above them, so a name cannot be added to
Rem the excluded list without saying under which rule — which is the
Rem whole difference between a decision and an omission.
Type Rule
    RuleWhy   As String
    RuleNames As List Of String

Def Excluded() As List Of Rule
    { RuleHandle(), RuleSocket(), RuleDevice(), RuleEffect(), RuleNoCell(), RuleNoTry(), RuleSpelled() }

Rem Aborts on a failure nothing can ask about first, and has no guarded
Rem twin to be rebuilt over. READFILE, WRITEFILE and APPENDFILE are the
Rem contrast that makes this a rule rather than an excuse: all three
Rem abort too, and all three ARE seeded, because TRYREADFILE and
Rem TRYWRITEFILE exist to rebuild them on. There is no TRYDELETEFILE.
Rem FILEEXISTS narrows the window and does not close it — a file that
Rem exists can still be locked or unreadable when the delete lands, and
Rem then the session is gone. seedfile.shoddy reached the same
Rem conclusion first: it registers FILEEXISTS and not DELETEFILE.
Def RuleNoTry() As Rule
    Rule("aborts on a failure it cannot pre-flight, with no guarded twin to build on",
         { "DELETEFILE" })

Rem R3.5(b), the rule that has already kept isam, recio and the binary
Rem model formats out: a handle is a NUMBER capped at sixteen and shared
Rem across kinds, so an UNDO after opening one restores the stack while
Rem leaving the resource open and now unreachable.
Def RuleHandle() As Rule
    Rule("a handle would reach the stack (R3.5(b))",
         { "BOPEN", "BCLOSE", "SEEK", "BPOS", "BSIZE",
           "PUTNUM", "GETNUM", "PUTBOOL", "GETBOOL", "PUTSTR", "GETSTR" })

Rem The same rule, plus a capability the mill has to be started with:
Rem --allow-net is not something a session can obtain half way through.
Def RuleSocket() As Rule
    Rule("a socket handle, and a capability a running session cannot obtain",
         { "TCPCONNECT", "TCPLISTEN", "TCPACCEPT", "TCPSECURE", "TCPSEND",
           "TCPRECV", "TCPEOF", "TCPPOLL", "TCPPEER", "TCPCLOSE" })

Rem A live window or a sound device. Same category as turtle, plotter
Rem and buzzer, which halifax-core.shoddy already records as unseedable.
Def RuleDevice() As Rule
    Rule("a live window or sound device the shell has no source for",
         { "SCRIBBLEROPEN", "SCRIBBLERPIXEL", "SCRIBBLERFILL", "SCRIBBLERTEXT",
           "SCRIBBLERGETPIXEL", "SCRIBBLERWIDTH", "SCRIBBLERHEIGHT",
           "SCRIBBLERBLIT", "SCRIBBLERCLOSE", "SCRIBBLERTITLE", "SCRIBBLERSAVE",
           "SCRIBBLERPLACE", "SCRIBBLERPOLL", "SCRIBBLERWAIT",
           "SCRIBBLERSETINTERVAL",
           "SOUND", "NOTEON", "NOTEOFF", "SOUNDQUEUE", "SOUNDSTOP",
           "SOUNDGAIN", "SOUNDWAVE" })

Rem R3.2: the engine performs no effect, and evaluation answers LINES
Rem for a shell to print. A PRINT word registered as RckTouch would
Rem write outside the transaction — the tape would not record it,
Rem TAPESAVE would lose it, and the screen and the tape would disagree,
Rem which is the one thing the single-render rule exists to prevent.
Rem Reading is the shell's for the matching reason: it owns the prompt,
Rem and a word that blocks for input mid-line would be a second thing
Rem reading the same keyboard.
Def RuleEffect() As Rule
    Rule("the engine performs no effect, and the shell owns the terminal (R3.2)",
         { "PRINT", "INPUT", "INPUTLINE", "INKEY" })

Rem R4.16: there is no ARRAY cell and no RESULT cell at the keyboard, so
Rem these four have nothing to answer WITH. TOARRAY and TOLIST would be
Rem the identity. TRYREADFILE and TRYWRITEFILE answer a Result, which is
Rem not a Cell — their guarding is what READFILE and WRITEFILE would be
Rem registered OVER, exactly as seedfile.shoddy already builds READLINES
Rem on TryReadFile rather than registering TryReadFile itself. EACH
Rem sequences effects and there are none to sequence, so it is MAP and a
Rem DROP, which the dictionary already has both of.
Def RuleNoCell() As Rule
    Rule("no cell to answer with, or nothing left to do once effects are gone (R4.16)",
         { "TOARRAY", "TOLIST", "TRYREADFILE", "TRYWRITEFILE", "EACH" })

Rem Already reachable under the name the dictionary chose, and a seed
Rem that registered a second spelling would be a seed that made HELP
Rem answer twice. The precedent is seedmath leaving RAD to the core and
Rem seedbool leaving AND and NOT to it.
Rem
Rem THREE OF THESE WERE CAUGHT BY WRITING THE LIST DOWN. & joins two
Rem STRINGs and so does the core + (CutAdd joins them), RND is what
Rem seed-random registered as RANDOM, and SORT is what seed-seq
Rem registered as SORTL with a guard on mixed kinds. None is in the
Rem dictionary under its BUILTIN name, so all three compute as free —
Rem and all three would have been a second spelling of a word already
Rem there, which is the thing this rule exists to stop. LEN is the near
Rem miss that is NOT one: LENGTH is list-only and refuses a STRING, so
Rem a string length genuinely has no word.
Rem
Rem NOTE the two logarithms, which are crossed: the builtin LOG is the
Rem natural one and the dictionary spells that LN, while the dictionary
Rem word LOG is base ten and is the builtin LOG10. Both are reachable;
Rem only the names moved.
Def RuleSpelled() As Rule
    Rule("already in the dictionary under the name it chose",
         { "SQR", "LOG10", "&", "RND", "SORT" })

Rem ---- what was left, and is now seedbuiltin's --------------------------

Rem These thirty-two were the free list — neither claimed nor excluded —
Rem and seedbuiltin.shoddy is what was written against it. So the
Rem assertion has moved on with them: it is no longer "these are free"
Rem but "these are exactly what the builtin seed registers, and nothing
Rem is free any more". That is the stronger claim of the two, because it
Rem fails in both directions — a name dropped from the seed, and a name
Rem added to it that this file has not been told about.
Def SeededHere() As List Of String
    { "APPENDFILE", "ARGS", "ASC", "ASSERT", "ATN", "ATN2", "BETAI",
      "CALL", "CHR", "CLOCK", "DIM", "ERF", "ERROR", "GAMMAP",
      "INSTR", "ISEMPTY", "ISNUMERIC", "LEFT", "LEN", "MID", "NEGATE", "NTH",
      "PREPEND", "READFILE", "RIGHT", "SETNTH", "SLEEP", "STR",
      "TANH", "VAL", "VALOR", "WRITEFILE" }

Rem ---- set plumbing ---------------------------------------------------

Rem Both of these take a Rule by name rather than reaching into one
Rem inside a lambda: All and Map pass their element as a generic t, and
Rem a record accessor cannot resolve against a type that is not yet
Rem known — it partially applies into a quotation instead, and fails a
Rem long way from the mistake, exactly as fin.shoddy warns about Mod.
Def NameOk(nm As String) As Boolean
    (nm <> "") And (Upper(nm) = nm)

Def RuleNamesOf(r As Rule) As List Of String
    RuleNames(r)

Def RuleOk(r As Rule) As Boolean
    (RuleWhy(r) <> "") And (Not IsEmpty(RuleNames(r)))

Def ExcludedNames() As List Of String
    Flatten(Map(Excluded(), RuleNamesOf))

Def Missing(xs As List Of String, ys As List Of String) As List Of String
    Filter(xs, Fn(n) => Not Contains(ys, n))

Rem Two lists hold the same names when neither has one the other lacks
Rem and neither repeats. Order is not meaning here, so it is not tested.
Def Same(xs As List Of String, ys As List Of String) As Boolean
    IsEmpty(Missing(xs, ys)) And IsEmpty(Missing(ys, xs)) And Length(xs) = Length(ys)

Def Dupes(xs As List Of String) As List Of String
    Filter(xs, Fn(n) => CountIf(xs, Fn(m) => m = n) > 1)

Def Show(what As String, xs As List Of String)
    Print("  " & what & " (" & Str(Length(xs)) & ")")
    If Not IsEmpty(xs) Then
        Print("    " & Join(xs, " "))

Def Main()
    Let st = HxFresh()
    Let surface = Builtins()
    Let excl = ExcludedNames()
    Let claimed = Filter(surface, Fn(nm) => RckKnows(st, nm))
    Let free = Missing(Missing(surface, claimed), excl)

    Print("--- the ground truth is well formed before it is used ---")
    Assert(IsEmpty(Dupes(surface)), "NO BUILTIN IS LISTED TWICE")
    Assert(IsEmpty(Dupes(excl)), "AND NO EXCLUDED NAME IS COVERED BY TWO RULES")
    Assert(All(surface, NameOk), "EVERY BUILTIN IS NAMED IN UPPER CASE, AS THE DICTIONARY MATCHES THEM")
    Assert(All(Excluded(), RuleOk), "EVERY EXCLUSION RULE HAS A REASON AND AT LEAST ONE NAME UNDER IT")
    Rem A stale exclusion is worse than none: it reads as a decision
    Rem about a builtin that no longer exists.
    Assert(IsEmpty(Missing(excl, surface)), "AND EVERY EXCLUDED NAME IS STILL A BUILTIN — " & Join(Missing(excl, surface), " "))

    Print("--- an excluded builtin is absent, which is the point of excluding it ---")
    Rem The load-bearing assertion, and it runs BEFORE the count below
    Rem rather than after. A name that is both excluded and in the
    Rem dictionary breaks the partition too — it is counted twice — so
    Rem the arithmetic would catch it either way, and say only that the
    Rem numbers do not add up. These six say WHICH RULE was broken, which
    Rem is the whole difference between a failure and a diagnosis, so
    Rem they are asked first.
    Assert(NoneKnown(st, RuleHandle()), "NO HANDLE WORD REACHED THE DICTIONARY")
    Assert(NoneKnown(st, RuleSocket()), "NOR ANY SOCKET WORD")
    Assert(NoneKnown(st, RuleDevice()), "NOR ANY WINDOW OR SOUND WORD")
    Assert(NoneKnown(st, RuleEffect()), "NOR PRINT AND THE THREE WAYS OF READING")
    Assert(NoneKnown(st, RuleNoCell()), "NOR THE ONES WITH NO CELL TO ANSWER WITH")
    Assert(NoneKnown(st, RuleSpelled()), "NOR A SECOND SPELLING OF A WORD THE DICTIONARY ALREADY HAS")

    Print("--- the two parts cover the whole surface, once each ---")
    Assert(Length(claimed) + Length(excl) + Length(free) = Length(surface), "CLAIMED PLUS EXCLUDED PLUS FREE IS THE WHOLE DISPATCH, WITH NOTHING COUNTED TWICE")
    Print("  " & Str(Length(surface)) & " builtins = " & Str(Length(claimed)) & " claimed + " & Str(Length(excl)) & " excluded + " & Str(Length(free)) & " free")
    Rem The whole point of the exercise, in one line: there is no third
    Rem part any more. Every builtin the runtime dispatches is either a
    Rem word a session can type or a name with a stated reason for not
    Rem being one.
    Assert(IsEmpty(free), "NOTHING IS UNACCOUNTED FOR — " & Join(free, " "))

    Print("--- and seedbuiltin registers exactly the list it was written from ---")
    Rem This is the collision test, and it now runs in both directions
    Rem against the seed itself rather than against a gap. A word dropped
    Rem from seedbuiltin, a word added to it, or a word of its stolen by
    Rem some later seed all show up here, by name.
    Assert(IsEmpty(Missing(Seeded(st), SeededHere())), "SEEDBUILTIN REGISTERS NOTHING THIS FILE HAS NOT BEEN TOLD ABOUT — " & Join(Missing(Seeded(st), SeededHere()), " "))
    Assert(IsEmpty(Missing(SeededHere(), Seeded(st))), "AND NOTHING THIS FILE EXPECTS OF IT HAS GONE MISSING — " & Join(Missing(SeededHere(), Seeded(st)), " "))
    Assert(Same(Seeded(st), SeededHere()), "SO THE SEED AND THE LIST AGREE EXACTLY")
    Rem And every one of them is a BUILTIN, not a word the seed invented:
    Rem seedbuiltin bridges the runtime, so a name of its own would mean
    Rem it had grown a domain.
    Assert(IsEmpty(Missing(Seeded(st), surface)), "AND EVERY WORD IT REGISTERS IS A BUILTIN, NOT ONE IT INVENTED — " & Join(Missing(Seeded(st), surface), " "))

    Print("--- what a session can now reach ---")
    Show("seeded by seedbuiltin", Seeded(st))

    Print("--- spot checks, so the partition is not merely self-consistent ---")
    Rem If HxFresh were somehow empty these assertions all pass and mean
    Rem nothing, so a few names are checked against what they must be.
    Assert(Contains(claimed, "DUP") And Contains(claimed, "+") And Contains(claimed, "MAP"), "THE CORE'S OWN WORDS COUNT AS CLAIMED")
    Assert(Contains(claimed, "FILEEXISTS"), "AND A BUILTIN A SEED REGISTERED COUNTS TOO")
    Assert(Contains(claimed, "LEN") And Contains(claimed, "VAL") And Contains(claimed, "NTH"), "AND THE COMMONEST GAPS ARE GAPS NO LONGER")
    Assert(Not RckKnows(st, "BOPEN"), "AND BOPEN IS STILL NOT A WORD IN THIS DICTIONARY")
    Assert(Length(claimed) > 40, "THE DICTIONARY IS REALLY THERE — WELL PAST FORTY BUILTINS ARE REACHABLE")

    Print("")
    Print("ALL ASSERTIONS PASSED")

Rem Every name under one rule, absent from the dictionary.
Def NoneKnown(st As RckState, r As Rule) As Boolean
    All(RuleNames(r), Fn(n) => Not RckKnows(st, n))

Rem What seedbuiltin actually put in the dictionary, read back out of it
Rem by the group name RckSeeding gave them. Read rather than declared, so
Rem the comparison above is against the seed and not against a second
Rem copy of the seed's intentions.
Def Seeded(st As RckState) As List Of String
    Map(Filter(RckDict(st), BiGroup), WordName)

Def BiGroup(e As RckEntry) As Boolean
    WordSeed(e) = "builtin"
